{"status":"ready","content":"I need to find the determinant of this 4×4 matrix. Let me denote the matrix as A and calculate its determinant using cofactor expansion.\n\nFirst, let me write the matrix more clearly:\n$$A = \\begin{pmatrix}\n\\frac{4}{3} & 1 & -\\frac{9}{2} & -\\frac{13}{3} \\\\\n-\\frac{13}{3} & \\frac{25}{6} & -\\frac{19}{6} & \\frac{11}{6} \\\\\n-\\frac{23}{6} & \\frac{29}{6} & -\\frac{10}{3} & -\\frac{19}{6} \\\\\n-\\frac{17}{6} & -\\frac{7}{6} & \\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{7}{3}\n\\end{pmatrix}$$\n\nTo make calculations easier, I'll multiply the entire matrix by 6 to clear most fractions, then divide the final result by $6^4 = 1296$.\n\n$$6A = \\begin{pmatrix}\n8 & 6 & -27 & -26 \\\\\n-26 & 25 & -19 & 11 \\\\\n-23 & 29 & -20 & -19 \\\\\n-17 & -7 & 15 & 14\n\\end{pmatrix}$$\n\nNow I'll calculate $\\det(6A)$ using cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$\\det(6A) = 8C_{11} + 6C_{12} + (-27)C_{13} + (-26)C_{14}$\n\nWhere $C_{ij}$ are the cofactors.\n\n$C_{11} = (-1)^{1+1} \\begin{vmatrix}\n25 & -19 & 11 \\\\\n29 & -20 & -19 \\\\\n-7 & 15 & 14\n\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nLet me calculate this 3×3 determinant:\n$= 25(-20 \\cdot 14 - (-19) \\cdot 15) - (-19)(29 \\cdot 14 - (-19) \\cdot (-7)) + 11(29 \\cdot 15 - (-20) \\cdot (-7))$\n$= 25(-280 + 285) + 19(406 - 133) + 11(435 - 140)$\n$= 25(5) + 19(273) + 11(295)$\n$= 125 + 5187 + 3245 = 8557$\n\n$C_{12} = (-1)^{1+2} \\begin{vmatrix}\n-26 & -19 & 11 \\\\\n-23 & -20 & -19 \\\\\n-17 & 15 & 14\n\\end{vmatrix}$\n\n$= -[(-26)(-20 \\cdot 14 - (-19) \\cdot 15) - (-19)((-23) \\cdot 14 - (-19) \\cdot (-17)) + 11((-23) \\cdot 15 - (-20) \\cdot (-17))]$\n$= -[(-26)(-280 + 285) + 19(-322 - 323) + 11(-345 - 340)]$\n$= -[(-26)(5) + 19(-645) + 11(-685)]$\n$= -[-130 - 12255 - 7535] = -[-19920] = 19920$\n\n$C_{13} = (-1)^{1+3} \\begin{vmatrix}\n-26 & 25 & 11 \\\\\n-23 & 29 & -19 \\\\\n-17 & -7 & 14\n\\end{vmatrix}$\n\n$= (-26)(29 \\cdot 14 - (-19) \\cdot (-7)) - 25((-23) \\cdot 14 - (-19) \\cdot (-17)) + 11((-23) \\cdot (-7) - 29 \